      <memberdef kind="function" id="class_u_tryll_languages_1a0d49c1f5f13dddd1412561d2b3f7221e" prot="public" static="yes" const="no" explicit="no" inline="no" virt="non-virtual">
        <type>FString</type>
        <definition>static FString UTryllLanguages::LanguageDisplayName</definition>
        <argsstring>(const FString &amp;Code)</argsstring>
        <name>LanguageDisplayName</name>
        <qualifiedname>UTryllLanguages::LanguageDisplayName</qualifiedname>
        <param>
          <type>const FString &amp;</type>
          <declname>Code</declname>
        </param>
        <briefdescription>
        </briefdescription>
        <detaileddescription>
<para>English name for a language code - &quot;de&quot; -&gt; &quot;German&quot; - for labelling a menu. Unknown codes come back unchanged, so an entry is never blank.</para>
<para>Deliberately a fixed table rather than FInternationalization: it must match what the server renders into the {{language}} prompt key exactly (see SessionLocale.cpp). Keep the two lists in sync when adding a language. </para>
        </detaileddescription>
        <inbodydescription>
        </inbodydescription>
        <location file="C:/_tryll/_monorepo3/tryll/clients/unreal/Source/TryllClient/Public/TryllLanguages.h" line="90" column="16"/>
      </memberdef>
    </sectiondef>
    <briefdescription>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para>Works out which languages your game can actually offer, from the models it actually uses.</para>
<para>The rule is an intersection: a language is offerable only if <emphasis>every</emphasis> model in the pipeline covers it. A game with a 31-language voice but an English-only recognizer can offer exactly one language for hands-free play - and the point of this library is that you find that out from the catalog instead of from a bug report.</para>
<para>Blueprint-callable, so a settings menu can populate itself without C++. </para>
    </detaileddescription>
